     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Confirming it again doesn't change the problem. 12,750 mg/day of metformin is about 5 times the maximum daily dose, and it could cause life-threatening lactic acidosis. I can't think of a clinical situation where this dose would be appropriate for a 70 kg adult.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n\n- **Check with your resident or attending.** If they really do want this dose, they can enter it themselves or tell you why. They can also confirm it with pharmacy.\n- **Re-check the source.** Look at the original note, handoff, or calculation. A decimal or unit error is the most likely explanation, for example 425 mg instead of 4250 mg, or a total daily dose entered as a per-dose amount.\n- **Check for an overdose.** If you're worried the patient has already taken a large amount of metformin, treat it as a possible overdose. Contact poison control and the senior team right away.\n\nOnce you have a verified dose, such as 500 mg or 850 mg three times daily with meals, tell me and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
